  "strategy": "## Approach and rationale  \nThe current optimization experiment uses the Erd\u0151s problem granularity and a continuous function representation. It can get stuck in local optima due to its genetic algorithm-based structure. We reframe the problem using piecewise constant representations, extending the analysis from 512 to 1024 sample points to strengthen the objective function's accuracy. This improves global dependency management. We add systematic pulse placement evolution to control the continuous variable space. The new approach explicitly handles the step-like nature of the Erd\u0151s solution through adaptive pulse locations and lengths, reflecting known optimal solutions.\n\n### Use Case Changes:  \n- Using a piecewise constant function minimizes grid effects  \n- Doubling the sample count increases precision  \n- Explicit pulse management reduces entanglement with grid indices\n\n### Relation to Previous:  \nBoth use bin counting, but the new approach does so systematically while using pulse amplitude instead of raw counts. The gradient estimation unrolls the computation to maintain linear complexity.\n\n## Mathematical specification  \n### Objective Function  \nC5 is defined as:  \n$$  \nC_5 = \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot n_{\\text{points}}} \\cdot \\max_{k} \\int_{0}^{2} h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx  \n$$  \nn_points = 1024  \ndx = 2.0 / n_points  \n\n### Constraints  \n1. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200 x  \n2. \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1  \n3. Discrete representation: Let h be a piecewise constant step function with amplitude `a` applied to specific intervals.\n\n### Pulse Representation  \nThe function h is defined by M pulses with centers c_i, lengths w_i, and amplitudes a_i. The constraint is:  \n\n$$  \n\\sum_{i=1}^{M} a_i \\cdot w_i \\cdot (\\text{effective length factor}) = 1  \n$$  \n\n### Gradient Calculation  \nThe gradient of C5 is approximated via:  \n\n$$  \n\\nabla h = \\left( \\frac{\\partial C_5}{\\partial h} \\right) \\approx \\text{pulse shift responses}  \n$$  \n\nWe use a unrolling method to keep complexity linear in the number of pulses.  \n\n## Implementation procedure  \n### Initialization  \n- Sample pulse positions and lengths uniformly:  \n  c_i = sorted(np.linspace(0, 2, M))  \n- Set length factor l = 2.0 / M  \n- Determine amplitudes to satisfy \u222b = 1.  \n- Then project into feasible space [0, 1].  \n\n### Pulse Management  \n1. Maintain fixed number of pulses M (default=4)  \n2. Pulse locations: uniformly distributed  \n3. Pulse lengths: proportional to M^(-0.3) \n\n### Optimization Algorithm  \nAdaptive_gradient_emulator(h, beta=100):  \n- Compute gradient estimation via pulse overlaps  \n- Adjust pulse parameters using coordinate ascent  \n- Clip amplitude to [0,1]  \n- Update pulse centers with logarithmic boundary conditioning  \n\n### Iteration Loop  \nfor iter in range(200_000):  \n  1. Estimate gradient  \n  2. Adjust pulse parameters  \n  3. Repair infeasibility  \n  4. Check termination condition  \n\n## Search choices and useful variations  \n### Default Configuration  \n- Pulse count: 4  \n- Amplitude normalization: uniform pulse heights  \n- Temperature schedule: linear from 100 to 50  \n\n### Versions  \n- Version 1: Reduce pulse count by 1 (pulse_param_version=1)  \n- Version 2: Increase pulse length factor (pulse_param_version=2)  \n- Version 3: Use logarithmic pulse positioning  \n\n## Compute budget and robustness  \n### Time Estimation  \n- Gradient estimation: O(n_points)  \n- Optimization iteration: O(log(pulse_count))  \n- Total wall time: ~650 seconds  \n\n### Restart Policy  \n- Save best C\u2085 every 1000 iterations  \n- Trigger restart if plateau length > 500 iterations  \n- Use logarithmic cooling schedule  \n\n## Validation and return contract  \n### Checks  \n1. Verify 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i  \n2. Check integration sum(h) \u2248 1 (tolerance=1e-3)  \n3. Compute C\u2085 using np.correlate via scaling  \n\n### Evaluation  \nnormalize_samples(h) \u2192 apply scaling factors to integrate to 1  \ncalculate_overlap(h) \u2192 correlation API use  \n\nBefore returning, ensure integration constraint with a fallback projection.",
